  "text": "The study examines how a beam of light changes direction when it passes close to a massive star like the Sun. Using observations made during a total eclipse, the researchers measured the apparent shift in the positions of distant stars whose light grazed the solar limb. By comparing these measurements with the expected positions in the absence of the star’s gravity, they quantified the amount of bending. The results matched the predictions of the prevailing theory of gravitation, confirming that massive objects can curve the path of light traveling nearby. This work provides empirical support for the idea that gravity influences not only matter but also electromagnetic radiation."
